REGENERATED WITH --instantiate=used --no_auto_instantiation
-----------------------------------------------------------
The committed C is now a SECOND translation, from the same commit and
the same cpfe recipe plus two flags.  What it fixes is the 556
template instantiations 6l reported undefined.

  WHY THEY WERE MISSING, from EDG's own source rather than from a
  guess: `DEFAULT_INSTANTIATION_MODE' resolves to `tim_none' here,
  because the build never defines INSTANTIATE_TEMPLATES_EVERYWHERE_USED
  -- and host_envir.h's own comment says the mode controls what is
  instantiated "without a specific request from the prelinker".
  DEFAULT_AUTOMATIC_INSTANTIATION_MODE is TRUE.  So cpfe was told to
  instantiate NOTHING and wait for an edg_prelink phase that this
  build never runs.  *That is a configuration assuming a two-phase
  build, not a limitation of the C back end* -- and it retires the
  recorded worry that `--one_instantiation_per_object' would be what
  introduced a prelink dependency: the default already had one.

  -tused emits every used template in every translation unit with
  EXTERNAL linkage, so it produces duplicates -- which is exactly what
  `pcc -C' now discards.  The two go together and neither is
  sufficient alone.  (-tlocal would give internal-linkage copies and
  need no -C, at a copy per unit; -C is needed either way for the
  non-template duplicates, which no instantiation mode touches.)

  MEASURED ON ONE FILE FIRST, because a full regeneration that changed
  nothing would have cost a round to discover.  src/interpret.c:

                          undefined templates   defined _Z
    committed recipe              39               226
    with the two flags             6               261

  and `edg::count_ones<unsigned int>' -- the exact symbol 6l named --
  goes from U to T.  All six survivors are DEFINED in other
  translation units (ordinary cross-TU references whose parameter
  types are templates), so the gap closes across the set.

  THEN THE WHOLE SET, the same sweep that reported 679:

                      before    after
    external            679       123
    _Z-prefixed         558         2
    template insts      556         0

  133 of 133 compile with gcc, 0 failures.  The two surviving _Z are
  the same non-template pair as before, a TLS init thunk and
  `operator new(size_t, align_val_t, nothrow_t const&)'.
  And `setjmp' rather than `_setjmp' now appears in the undefined set,
  so kencc_targ.h's TARG_SETJMP_FUNC line is confirmed and that gap
  closed with the same run.

  lib_src/ KEEPS ITS RECORDED RECIPE EXACTLY and is regenerated only
  so TARG_SETJMP_FUNC reaches eh_util.c and newnothrow.c.  It had no
  missing instantiations, `--building_runtime' is a different mode,
  and adding -tused there would be an unmeasured change to the
  runtime.  util/ gets the flags, being the same kind of program.

  util/cfe_daemon_client.c still does not translate and it is still
  the only one -- 133 produced, 1 failure, predicted by name before
  the run.

  AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT MEASURED NOTHING, for a reason worth keeping:
  the driver ran its workers under `xargs ... bash -c', and **bash
  cannot export an array**.  `export INC CPPDEF' was silently a no-op,
  every `--sys_include' vanished inside the subshell, and all 134
  files died at `bits/libc-header-start.h' -- glibc's headers, exit 4.
  The single-file test had passed minutes earlier in the shell that
  DEFINED the arrays.  *An instrument whose environment is not the
  build's environment is measuring a different program*, which is the
  include-path lesson arriving for a shell rather than a compiler.

THE LIBAP HALF OF THE LINK IS ANSWERED, AND IT IS TWO NAMES
-----------------------------------------------------------
Asked all at once rather than one per round, which is the rule bacon's
missing headers and bacon's missing link symbols both paid for: all
133 files compiled with `gcc -c' (133 of 133, 0 failures), then
`nm --defined-only -g' over every object subtracted from `nm -u' over
every object.

  symbols defined across the 133      22963
  undefined mentions                   9667
  TRULY EXTERNAL to the set             679
    of which `_Z'-prefixed              558   <- the link model, below
    of which plain C names              121

**Of the 121 plain names, libap already defines every one but two.**
Each was checked by opening the file, not by a grep hit -- and the
first sweep reported two dozen absent because its regex required a
character before the name, which a definition starting in column 0
does not have.  *That is the function-shaped grep of the `reject'
round in a new spelling, and it would have manufactured two dozen
findings.*  Present and verified: mmap/munmap (plan9/mman.c),
get/setrlimit, popen/pclose, sysconf, setlocale, posix_memalign,
mkstemp, pthread_key_create/setspecific, clock/ctime/difftime, isatty,
getcwd, isprint and the whole of stdio/string.

  _setjmp         lib_src/eh_util.c, lib_src/newnothrow.c
  __cxa_finalize  lib_src/static_init.c

`_setjmp' is a MISSED LINE IN kencc_targ.h rather than a libap gap:
EDG's `TARG_SETJMP_FUNC' defaults to "_setjmp" and win64 -- the same
configuration every other value here came from -- says "setjmp".  Set
now, so it is right at the next translation; the header records what
the miss costs and why adding `_setjmp' to a public APE header is the
wrong answer (lua/ldo.c already uses the name under LUA_USE_POSIX).

`__cxa_finalize' is NOT a libap question at all.  It is unconditional
in EDG's own `include_c++/cxxabi.h:184', nothing in lib_src defines
it, and `_Z12__call_dtorsv''s entire body is `__cxa_finalize(0)' --
upstream expects the platform's Itanium-ABI runtime to supply it.  On
a target that has none, the answer is one small APExp-owned file in
`sys/src/ape/lib/edg', where the C++ ABI belongs.  Not written: only
the link says whether cpfe references it.

Two more names are HOST ARTEFACTS and cannot appear from pcc:
`__stack_chk_fail' and `_GLOBAL_OFFSET_TABLE_'.  And the whole
libgcc soft-float family -- `__addtf3', `__multf3', `__eqhf2',
`__extendbfsf2', `__trunctfdf2' and twenty more -- comes from
`lib_src/c99_complex.c' ALONE, which is already predicted to fail on
`_Float16'/`__bf16'/`__float80'/`__float128'.  *The extended-float
types bring a soft-float library with them, and kencc has no libgcc*,
so that file is doubly out of reach and the count is not two findings.

Two confirmations fell out of the same table, and both say the staging
really used this tree: `_errnoloc' is undefined in six of src/'s
files and DEFINED in libap (`plan9/_envsetup.c:22'), which is APE's
`errno' macro from `<errno.h>' having been expanded; and `isNaN' --
Plan 9's own capitalisation, from `ap/math/nan.c' -- is referenced by
`src/float_pt.c'.  Neither name exists on glibc.

THE REMAINING LINK MODEL IS THE OPEN PROBLEM, AND IT IS TWO HALVES
------------------------------------------------------------------
**The 558 `_Z' names are NOT the duplicate problem seen from the other
side, and reading them that way was the round's one wrong sentence.**
They were demangled and counted rather than assumed:

  556 of the 558 are TEMPLATE FUNCTION INSTANTIATIONS
      edg::count_ones<unsigned int>, edg::index_to_str<...>,
      edg::pdqsort_impl::pdqsort_loop<...>, edg::is_named_decl<...>
  ZERO are vtables (_ZTV), typeinfo (_ZTI), typeinfo names (_ZTS),
      guard variables (_ZGV) or thunks
  the other 2 are a TLS init function and
      operator new(size_t, align_val_t, nothrow_t const&)

`_ZN3edg10count_onesIjEEyT_' appears in `src/interpret.c' as a call
and an `extern' declaration and **is defined in none of the 133
files**.  So cpfe emitted these instantiations NOWHERE: the output is
not self-contained for templates, *independently of
`--one_instantiation_per_object'*.  The paragraph below says OIPO
"makes the output DEPEND on a prelink step", which is true and
incomplete -- the DEFAULT output already does, for exactly the
entities OIPO was being considered for.  What the next translation
needs is an instantiation-mode flag (EDG's `-tused'/`--instantiate'
family) so the definitions are emitted; that will move work from this
half into the duplicate half below, where `ar' and `6l' semantics
decide rather than a missing symbol.

AND THE DUPLICATE HALF IS NOW MEASURED FROM OBJECTS RATHER THAN FROM
ANNOTATIONS, which is what makes the 3,094 below trustworthy:

  symbols DEFINED in more than one of the 133 objects   3124
  surplus definitions (sum of copies - 1)               6410
    _ZN... (inlines and emitted template members)       2616
    _ZZ... (function-local statics, incl. string lits)   499
    _ZTI/_ZTS (typeinfo and its name)                      4
    plain C names                                          5

3,124 against the 3,094 counted from COMDAT annotations is an
independent arrival at the same number by a different route, which is
better evidence than either alone.  `edg::skip_typerefs' is emitted
**41 times**, which is the shape: an inline reachable from a common
header.

  THE FIVE PLAIN NAMES ARE `main', `getopt', `optind', `optarg' and
  `opterr', and all five are confined to util/ -- `edg_decode',
  `edg_munch' and `edg_prelink' each bundle their own getopt, and the
  seven mains are src/cfe.c plus util's six programs, exactly as the
  file counts said.  **So nothing in src/ or lib_src/ is affected**,
  and cmd/edg compiles only src/.  But it is recorded because util/ is
  on the list: libap provides `getopt' and the three variables, so
  linking any of those three utilities against libap gives
  `redefinition: getopt' -- the f2c-supplies-its-own-malloc shape, and
  the reason an object on `_apemain''s path must define nothing a
  program might reasonably replace.

Removing __weak__ does not make the vague-linkage problem go away; it
stops hiding it.  Measured on two trivial translation units sharing
one header: with the kencc target they compile and then collide on 10
symbols -- B::f, three B::~B, typeinfo, typeinfo name, vtable, and
three Box<int> members.

Across src/ as shipped: 36,111 COMDAT annotations, 11,533 distinct
COMDAT symbols, and **3,094 of those are named in more than one of the
75 files**.  Those are what a kencc link has to resolve.  (An upper
bound on colliding definitions -- some annotations are declarations.)

`--one_instantiation_per_object` IS NOT USED HERE, deliberately.
Measured on the same two TUs, it removes the TEMPLATE instantiations
from the duplicate set (3 of the 10) and leaves the inline members,
vtable and typeinfo of an ordinary class (the other 7).  It does that
by NOT emitting them and listing them in a .ti file instead, for
`edg_prelink` to assign to exactly one object and schedule a separate
compile.  So OIPO is not a flag that makes the output self-contained:
it makes the output DEPEND on a prelink step.

  What that would mean for a Plan 9 mk build, stated and not built:
  mk would need a two-phase rule per package -- translate every .c,
  collect the .ti files, run edg_prelink over the whole set to decide
  which object owns each instantiation, then compile the extra
  generated files it names and only then link.  mk has no natural way
  to express "the set of objects is not known until after the first
  phase"; it would want a recursive mk or a generated mkfile
  fragment.  edg_prelink and edg_munch are both in util/ here, so the
  pieces exist.  NOT ATTEMPTED.

  The other half -- inline members, vtables, typeinfo of ordinary
  classes -- is NOT covered by OIPO at all.  Upstream's answer for a
  target without COMDAT is the Cfront-like ABI: host_envir.h:862's
  LINKER_CAN_DISCARD_DUPLICATE_DEFINITIONS, whose own comment is "a
  simplified interpretation of this flag is: do we have COMDAT
  sections?", forced TRUE by IA64_ABI.
  THAT WAS ATTEMPTED AND BACKED OUT, and where it stops is worth
  knowing: with IA64_ABI=0 plus the two settings its own #errors ask
  for (TIE_DEFAULT_GNU_ABI_VERSION_TO_GNU_VERSION,
  HANDLE_VIRTUAL_BASES_IN_COMPLETE_CTOR_DTORS), cpfe fails to COMPILE
  in src/target_map.h on three macros the Cfront ABI needs and the
  IA-64 ABI does not:
      TARG_ALIGNOF_PTR_TO_VIRTUAL_BASE_CLASS_linux_i686
      TARG_SIZEOF_PTR_TO_VIRTUAL_BASE_CLASS_linux_i686
      TARG_RUNTIME_ELEM_COUNT_INT_KIND_linux_i686
  This build is MULTI-TARGET -- the platform cmakedef declares
  TARGET_CONFIGURATION_1..7 and target.c instantiates the target map
  once per configuration -- and the generated cmake_defines.h carries
  the suffixed <MACRO>_<config> spellings only for macros reachable
  under IA64_ABI=1, which is what it was generated for.  Setting
  EDG_CPP_RT_LIBS="" does not help; that list is separate.
  Two of the three are mechanical (targ_def.h:2837,2842 derive them
  from TARG_SIZEOF_POINTER / TARG_ALIGNOF_POINTER) but the suffixed
  spellings must still be written out for all seven secondary targets,
  and TARG_RUNTIME_ELEM_COUNT_INT_KIND has no derived default at all.
  So the Cfront ABI is reachable by REGENERATING the macro
  configuration for it, not by setting switches -- and it is a large
  semantic change (mangling, ctor/dtor model, vtable layout) that
  wants its own round and its own validation.

APExp HEADER CHANGES THIS TRANSLATION DEPENDS ON
------------------------------------------------
At translation time the headers were STAGED (copied) and edited in the
staging copy only, and NOTHING IN APExp's HEADER TREE WAS MODIFIED.
Eight edits were needed, every one a case of an APE header not being
usable from C++.

**ALL EIGHT ARE FIXED IN THE TREE NOW, so a re-translation needs NONE
of them** -- stage the headers as they stand.  The list is kept
because it is the record of what a stricter compiler found, and
because each was fixed on its own evidence rather than to suit EDG.

The last four, each a parameter name or a keyword, so none of them
changes what a C caller sees:

  sys/include/ape/stdlib.h:47,49,87  `_Noreturn` (a C11 keyword that
      does not exist in C++) used unguarded
  sys/include/ape/stdlib.h:69,70     parameter named `template`
  sys/include/ape/unistd.h:186,192   parameter named `new`
  sys/include/ape/signal.h:161-164   `restrict` as a parameter name;
      two per prototype reads as a duplicate parameter name in C++

And the first four, fixed earlier in the same session for their own
reasons.  Verified against the tree rather than assumed:

  signal.h   `signal()` is prototyped now --
             `extern void (*signal(int, void (*)(int)))(int)` at :86
  signal.h   SIG_DFL/SIG_ERR/SIG_IGN are `(void (*)(int))` at :45-47;
             they had to move with the declaration, since callers
             compare the return value against them
  features.h `#define hidden` is GONE from the public header -- it had
             a measured victim in `sqlite3.h:10957`'s
             `unsigned char hidden[48]`
  bsd.h:53   `getopt(int, char * const [], const char *)`, POSIX's
             spelling, agreeing with `<getopt.h>` at last

*That is the translation's own staging list working as a to-do list
for the HEADER TREE*: it was written as eight things EDG could not
use, and every one turned out to be a defect worth fixing on its own
evidence, found by a compiler stricter than gcc.

The control is a before-and-after pair on IDENTICAL staging: a C++
translation unit including <stdlib.h>, <unistd.h> and <signal.h>
gives 12 errors before and 1 after.  The same unit as C is 0 errors,
which is the half that could have cost the tree.

THE SURVIVOR IS A NINTH ITEM THIS LIST NEVER NAMED: stddef.h:50
typedefs `wchar_t', which is a BUILT-IN TYPE in C++.  It is present
in both runs, so it is pre-existing rather than anything done here,
and it is recorded and not fixed.  cpfe does not trip on it --
`--no_standard_includes' plus EDG's own include_c99 is why -- so it
blocks nothing in this directory.

Two of the four could not be fixed the obvious way.  `#define
_Noreturn' or `#define restrict' under __cplusplus would put an
UNRESERVED name in a public APE header that external packages define
themselves: gnulib ships a whole `_Noreturn.h' and a dozen `config.h'
here define `restrict'.  That is exactly the collision that broke
flex over `reject' one round earlier, so the spellings used are all
in the reserved double-underscore space.  Three of the four need no
macro at all -- `restrict' becomes `__restrict', which kencc lexes to
the same LRESTRICT (cc/lex.c:1652,1678-1679), and the two parameters
are simply renamed.  `__ape_template' is this directory's own
convention arriving back in the tree: the generated C already carries
it.

One absolute include also had to be rewritten for a host compile:
amd64/include/ape/stddef_arch.h's `#include "/sys/include/ape/..."`.
Four others of the form `#include "/sys/include/..."` name native
Plan 9 headers and are unreachable from EDG, so they were left.

The four that remain are reported as findings in their own right and
are not fixed here; they are not EDG's problem either.
